WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.324 --> 00:00:01.960
(pleasant synth music)

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.420
- Scientists at the National
Institutes of Health

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.980
are working to create a
vaccine for the new coronavirus

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.930
that originated in China,

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.000
but they're not starting from scratch.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.000
They're employing an approach

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.380
called Vaccine Rapid Response Platforms.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.850
It involves high-tech methods
that have the potential

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.330
to shave years off of development time.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.770
Ideally, they'd offer protection

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.503
while an epidemic is still
spreading instead of years later.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.420
Within weeks of the world
learning about the Wuhan outbreak,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.280
Chinese scientists uploaded
the novel coronavirus'

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.150
genetic sequence to a public database.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.560
That allowed teams around the world

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.490
to start designing a vaccine.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:43.220
Traditional methods require
an actual sample of the virus.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.560
Typically, scientists inactivate a virus

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.260
using special chemicals before
it's put into a vaccine.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.270
When the inactivated or weakened virus

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.360
is injected into the body, the
immune system recognizes it

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.040
as a foreign invader or an antigen.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.600
Vaccines use antigens to prime the body

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.970
to protect against a particular virus,

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.330
but vaccines developed

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.750
with Rapid Response
Platforms work differently.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.170
Rather than directly injecting
antigens into the body,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.080
these types of vaccines
typically send instructions

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.630
to cells in the body.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:17.570
It gets the cells to
produce antigen proteins

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:21.130
that are specific to the
virus it's designed to defeat.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.630
These instructions are in
the form of RNA or DNA,

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.850
the molecules that contain the
code for building proteins.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.550
Scientists say this process
cuts down on development time

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:33.130
because they don't have
to grow the whole virus.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.170
Plus, once scientists identify
and create the instructions

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.060
for one virus, they can
tweak those instructions

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.400
to make a vaccine for a similar virus.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.260
- So think of the backbone
as a cassette player

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.160
and the new virus sequence of our target,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.490
like from the new coronavirus,

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.820
that sequence that we design,

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.360
and think of it as a new cassette tape,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.110
so we can just slot it in.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.140
- You can slot in new virus antigens.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.900
- [Jason] Melanie Saville is the Director

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.570
of Vaccine Research and Development

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.440
at the Coalition for
Epidemic Preparedness.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.380
CEPI is a global partnership

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.380
that was launched at Davos in 2017.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.770
- We were actually formed specifically

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.690
to look at this type of situation.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:18.750
- [Jason] In this situation,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.840
the emergence of a new coronavirus,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.380
CEPI's funding several
teams from around the world

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:26.210
that Saville says are
each independently working

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:27.890
from the platform model.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.410
- The sort of technologies
that we are looking at,

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.510
some of which are really quite pioneering,

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.037
can really move very quickly.

00:02:34.037 --> 00:02:35.960
(pleasant orchestral music)

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:36.830
- [Jason] Three of the candidates

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:40.100
are in a category called
nucleic acid vaccines.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.140
One of those candidates is Moderna,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.480
which is working with the NIH.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.360
They're developing a vaccine on a platform

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.700
that uses a part of
the virus' genetic code

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.970
called messenger RNA or mRNA,

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:54.490
so again, unlike the
conventional approach,

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.970
with mRNA vaccine platforms,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:00.480
it's our own body's cells
rather than lab techs

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.970
that produce the antigen proteins

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.730
that are like the ones
made by the coronavirus.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.920
- Your immune system
then will be stimulated

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.110
and develop antibodies to the virus

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:13.690
so that when you see it again,

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.570
it can immediately recognize the virus

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:19.660
and prevent you from becoming sick.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.260
- [Jason] More and more
people are becoming sick

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.240
from the novel coronavirus.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.940
As the race for a viable
vaccine continues,

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.890
public health officials don't know how bad

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.660
the situation will be
by the time they hope

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.513
to have a vaccine ready for
widespread distribution.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.420
- Normally, vaccines would take years

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.490
to get into the clinic, so
the sort of technologies

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:42.410
that we are looking at can
really move very quickly.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.200
So an example of that is
getting from identifying

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.930
the sequence of the virus
to developing a vaccine,

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.360
doing all of the pre-clinical testing,

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.040
manufacturing, and getting
into the clinic in 16 weeks.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.070
- [Jason] So, around four months.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:02.320
During the 2003 SARS outbreak,
that process took 20 months.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.330
Experts say if one of the
four teams CEPI is funding

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:09.780
is successful, it would a
watershed moment for human health.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.810
- These are very promising candidates,

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.330
but they're still quite early days.

